1
00:00:00,140 --> 00:00:01,140
Watch this.

2
00:00:01,140 --> 00:00:05,780
I send !haunt mug in real Twitch chat.

3
00:00:05,780 --> 00:00:10,940
Haunt My Desk receives the message through
Twitch EventSub and queues it for the host.

4
00:00:10,940 --> 00:00:18,980
The host has marked a real red mug in the
camera frame, and nothing appears in OBS until
the host approves it.

5
00:00:18,980 --> 00:00:23,100
Two AI roles review a challenge from a safe
catalog.

6
00:00:23,100 --> 00:00:28,220
Challenge Maker proposes the task, and Safety
Referee checks it.

7
00:00:28,220 --> 00:00:32,340
A fixed allowlist and the host make the final
decision.

8
00:00:32,340 --> 00:00:38,060
Once approved, OBS places a spell card and a
25-second timer over my camera.

9
00:00:38,060 --> 00:00:43,060
I wave my hand above the mug without touching
it, and then I mark the spell broken.

10
00:00:43,060 --> 00:00:46,260
The viewer's Twitch name appears in the result.

11
00:00:46,260 --> 00:00:53,620
I built the chat listener, queue, host studio,
transparent OBS overlay, and tests with Codex.

12
00:00:53,620 --> 00:00:57,260
During real camera testing, we found and fixed
an overlay transparency bug.

13
00:00:57,260 --> 00:01:04,180
This is a local OBS recording triggered by a
real Twitch message. The public showcase is
separately labeled as a simulation.

14
00:01:04,180 --> 00:01:11,180
Haunt My Desk turns chat into a physical moment
viewers can watch.
